{
  "id": "dictionaries",
  "title": "Dictionaries",
  "reflection_prompt": "In your own words, when would you reach for a dictionary instead of a list?",
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "01",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Create and Print a Dictionary",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "sidebar_tag": "dict",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a <code>name</code> and an <code>age</code>, build a",
        "dictionary with keys <code>\"name\"</code> and <code>\"age\"</code>,",
        "and print it.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "Ava",
        "16",
        "{'name': 'Ava', 'age': 16}</pre>",
        "<p>A dictionary stores <strong>key-value pairs</strong> using curly",
        "braces: <code>{\"name\": \"Ava\", \"age\": 16}</code>. Unlike a list,",
        "you look things up by a meaningful key instead of a numeric",
        "position.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "name = input()",
        "age = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: build a dict with keys \"name\" and \"age\", then print it"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Ava",
            "16"
          ],
          "expected": "{'name': 'Ava', 'age': 16}"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Marcus",
            "17"
          ],
          "expected": "{'name': 'Marcus', 'age': 17}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "02",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Access by Key",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "sidebar_tag": "indexing",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a <code>name</code> and an <code>age</code> into a",
        "dictionary, then print just the value stored under",
        "<code>\"name\"</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "Ava",
        "16",
        "Ava</pre>",
        "<p><code>person[\"name\"]</code> looks up the value associated with",
        "the key <code>\"name\"</code> &mdash; similar to indexing a list, but",
        "with a key instead of a position.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "name = input()",
        "age = int(input())",
        "person = {\"name\": name, \"age\": age}",
        "",
        "# TODO: print person[\"name\"]"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Ava",
            "16"
          ],
          "expected": "Ava"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Priya",
            "15"
          ],
          "expected": "Priya"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Add or Update a Key",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "sidebar_tag": "assignment",
      "description": [
        "<p>Start with a dictionary containing just a <code>\"name\"</code>.",
        "Read an <code>age</code>, then add it to the dictionary under the key",
        "<code>\"age\"</code>, and print the result.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "Ava",
        "16",
        "{'name': 'Ava', 'age': 16}</pre>",
        "<p><code>person[\"age\"] = age</code> adds a new key if it doesn't",
        "exist yet, or updates the existing value if it does &mdash; the same",
        "syntax does both jobs.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "person = {\"name\": input()}",
        "",
        "# TODO: read an age, add it to person under the key \"age\",",
        "# then print person"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Ava",
            "16"
          ],
          "expected": "{'name': 'Ava', 'age': 16}"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "Marcus",
            "17"
          ],
          "expected": "{'name': 'Marcus', 'age': 17}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "04",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Key Exists Check",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "sidebar_tag": "in",
      "description": [
        "<p>Using the fixed dictionary <code>{\"name\": \"Ava\", \"age\": 16}</code>,",
        "read a key name and print <code>True</code> if it exists in the",
        "dictionary, or <code>False</code> otherwise.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "age",
        "True</pre>",
        "<p>For a dictionary, <code>in</code> checks the <strong>keys</strong>,",
        "not the values.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "person = {\"name\": \"Ava\", \"age\": 16}",
        "key = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print key in person"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "age"
          ],
          "expected": "True"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "height"
          ],
          "expected": "False"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "name"
          ],
          "expected": "True"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "05",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Building a Dictionary from Input",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "sidebar_tag": "for + dict",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, then read <code>n</code> key/value",
        "pairs &mdash; a key, then an integer value, one per line each &mdash;",
        "building them into a single dictionary. Print the result.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "2",
        "a",
        "1",
        "b",
        "2",
        "{'a': 1, 'b': 2}</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "d = {}",
        "",
        "# TODO: use a for loop over range(n) to read a key and an integer",
        "# value each time, and store them in d. Then print d."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "2",
            "a",
            "1",
            "b",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "{'a': 1, 'b': 2}"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "x",
            "10",
            "y",
            "20",
            "z",
            "30"
          ],
          "expected": "{'x': 10, 'y': 20, 'z': 30}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "06",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Loop Over Keys",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "sidebar_tag": "for",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, build a dictionary from",
        "<code>n</code> key/value pairs as before, then print each",
        "<strong>key</strong> on its own line, in the order they were",
        "added.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "a",
        "1",
        "b",
        "2",
        "c",
        "3",
        "a",
        "b",
        "c</pre>",
        "<p>Looping over a dictionary directly (<code>for key in d</code>)",
        "walks through its keys &mdash; and since Python 3.7, dictionaries",
        "reliably remember the order things were added in.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "d = {}",
        "",
        "# TODO: build d from input as before, then loop over d",
        "# and print each key on its own line"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "a",
            "1",
            "b",
            "2",
            "c",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "a\nb\nc"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "2",
            "x",
            "10",
            "y",
            "20"
          ],
          "expected": "x\ny"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "07",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Loop Over Values",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "sidebar_tag": "for + .values()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, build a dictionary from",
        "<code>n</code> key/value pairs, then print each",
        "<strong>value</strong> on its own line, using",
        "<code>.values()</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "a",
        "1",
        "b",
        "2",
        "c",
        "3",
        "1",
        "2",
        "3</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "d = {}",
        "",
        "# TODO: build d from input as before, then loop over d.values()",
        "# and print each value on its own line"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "a",
            "1",
            "b",
            "2",
            "c",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "1\n2\n3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "08",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Loop Over Items",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "sidebar_tag": "for + .items()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, build a dictionary from",
        "<code>n</code> key/value pairs, then print each pair in the format",
        "<code>key: value</code>, one per line, using <code>.items()</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "2",
        "a",
        "1",
        "b",
        "2",
        "a: 1",
        "b: 2</pre>",
        "<p><code>.items()</code> gives you both the key and value together on",
        "each pass through the loop.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "d = {}",
        "",
        "# TODO: build d from input as before, then loop over d.items()",
        "# printing an f-string: {key}: {value}"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "2",
            "a",
            "1",
            "b",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "a: 1\nb: 2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "09",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Counting with a Dictionary",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "sidebar_tag": "dict",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a sentence and print a dictionary that counts how many times",
        "each word appears.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "the cat the dog the",
        "{'the': 3, 'cat': 1, 'dog': 1}</pre>",
        "<p>For each word: if it's already a key in the dictionary, increase",
        "its count by 1; otherwise, add it as a new key with a count of",
        "1.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "words = input().split()",
        "counts = {}",
        "",
        "# TODO: for each word in words, increment counts[word] if word",
        "# is already a key, otherwise set counts[word] = 1.",
        "# Then print counts."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "the cat the dog the"
          ],
          "expected": "{'the': 3, 'cat': 1, 'dog': 1}"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "a a b b b c"
          ],
          "expected": "{'a': 2, 'b': 3, 'c': 1}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": ".get() with a Default",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "sidebar_tag": "get()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Using the fixed dictionary <code>{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}</code>, read a",
        "key and print its value &mdash; but print <code>0</code> instead of",
        "crashing if the key doesn't exist, using <code>.get()</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "z",
        "0</pre>",
        "<p><code>d.get(key, default)</code> looks up <code>key</code> and",
        "returns <code>default</code> instead of raising an error if the key",
        "isn't found &mdash; a safer alternative to <code>d[key]</code> when",
        "you're not sure the key exists.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}",
        "key = input()",
        "",
        "# TODO: print d.get(key, 0)"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "z"
          ],
          "expected": "0"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "a"
          ],
          "expected": "1"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "b"
          ],
          "expected": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "source_unit": "dictionaries",
      "title": "Updating a Value",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "sidebar_tag": "+=",
      "description": [
        "<p>Start with a dictionary <code>{\"player\": 0}</code>. Read a number",
        "of points and add it to the existing score, then print the",
        "dictionary.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "5",
        "{'player': 5}</pre>",
        "<p><code>scores[\"player\"] += points</code> updates the existing",
        "value in place &mdash; the same shortcut you've used before with",
        "plain variables works on dictionary values too.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "scores = {\"player\": 0}",
        "",
        "# TODO: read a number and add it to scores[\"player\"],",
        "# then print scores"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "{'player': 5}"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10"
          ],
          "expected": "{'player': 10}"
        }
      ]
    },
